WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
In this lesson, we'll go through parallel execution patterns. We'll see how fan-out works,

2
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.439
how to verify that branches are actually independent before dispatching them,

3
00:00:09.439 --> 00:00:14.479
how fan-in aggregates results at the synchronization point, what to do when some

4
00:00:14.479 --> 00:00:20.719
branches fail, and how the cost versus latency trade-off plays out in practice. Let's get into it.

5
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:26.079
Running work in parallel is the easiest way to cut an agent's wall clock time,

6
00:00:26.079 --> 00:00:31.920
and the easiest way to introduce bugs you can't reproduce. Parallel execution means multiple

7
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:37.840
independent tasks run concurrently. It needs a synchronization point to collect results and

8
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:43.520
handle partial failures before the pipeline continues. Fan-out is the dispatch step,

9
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:49.279
where a single task splits into multiple concurrent subtasks sent off all at once,

10
00:00:49.279 --> 00:00:54.159
and it only holds up if every branch is independent of the others. Fan-in is the

11
00:00:54.159 --> 00:00:59.200
synchronization step on the other side. It collects results from all the branches,

12
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.239
deals with varying result counts and partial failures, and reassembles everything before

13
00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:11.199
the pipeline moves forward. Fan-out is the dispatch step in a parallel execution pattern.

14
00:01:11.199 --> 00:01:17.040
One control point triggers multiple concurrent subtasks all at the same time. Every branch gets

15
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:22.879
its own inputs and runs independently. The branches don't have to be simple function calls.

16
00:01:22.879 --> 00:01:28.879
They can be full sub-agents, tool invocations, or nested workflows. The key constraint is that

17
00:01:28.879 --> 00:01:35.919
every branch has to be independent. Fan-out increases throughput by running work simultaneously

18
00:01:35.919 --> 00:01:40.959
rather than one at a time, but it only works correctly when the branches genuinely don't

19
00:01:40.959 --> 00:01:46.319
depend on each other. A fan-out with hidden interbranch dependencies produces incorrect

20
00:01:46.319 --> 00:01:52.800
results because branches might run in any order or read stale state written by a sibling branch

21
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:59.120
that ran concurrently. Before committing to a fan-out design, run the independence test on

22
00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:05.040
every branch pair. There are three questions to ask. First, can branch B start without waiting

23
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:11.839
for any output from branch A? If B needs A's output, they have a dependency and can't fan out.

24
00:02:11.839 --> 00:02:18.160
Second, do the branches write to shared state that could cause race conditions? If two branches

25
00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.759
both update the same record, the result depends on which one finishes last, and that's not

26
00:02:23.759 --> 00:02:29.919
deterministic. Third, would running the branches in any order produce the same final result?

27
00:02:29.919 --> 00:02:35.759
If order matters between branches, they're not independent. If any of these questions reveals a

28
00:02:35.759 --> 00:02:42.080
dependency, fan-out is the wrong pattern for those branches. Fix the dependency first or use a

29
00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:49.440
sequential design where ordering is enforced. Fan-in has four responsibilities at the synchronization

30
00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:56.639
point. First, it collects outputs from all branches that completed successfully. Second, it handles

31
00:02:56.639 --> 00:03:02.639
failures from branches that erred. Third, if the order of results matters for the next pipeline

32
00:03:02.639 --> 00:03:08.800
step, it reassembles outputs in the correct sequence. And fourth, it merges the collected

33
00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.160
results and passes the aggregated output to whatever comes next. Each of these has to be

34
00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:20.559
explicitly designed. An ad hoc fan-in that just takes whatever results are ready and throws away

35
00:03:20.559 --> 00:03:27.119
failures is a silent failure source. The fan-in logic is where partial failures show up, and

36
00:03:27.119 --> 00:03:32.399
handling them explicitly is a non-negotiable design requirement for parallel pipelines.

37
00:03:33.679 --> 00:03:39.839
Parallel and sequential execution have a real trade-off worth understanding clearly. Parallel

38
00:03:39.839 --> 00:03:45.919
execution reduces total wall clock time when branches are genuinely independent. Multiple

39
00:03:45.919 --> 00:03:52.320
branches run simultaneously, but it costs more in token spend per unit time and introduces

40
00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:58.160
partial failure scenarios that sequential execution doesn't have. Sequential execution

41
00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:04.000
is slower. Total time equals the sum of all step latencies, but it's cheaper per run,

42
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.600
and there's no synchronization logic to write and no partial failures from concurrent branches.

43
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:15.839
The exam trap here is worth flagging. Parallel execution introduces partial failure scenarios

44
00:04:15.839 --> 00:04:20.880
that sequential execution doesn't. Designing explicit handling for the case where some

45
00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:26.000
branches succeed and others fail is a required part of any parallel pipeline design.

46
00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:33.519
When some parallel branches succeed and others fail, the fan-in step has three options. First,

47
00:04:33.519 --> 00:04:39.359
fail the whole pipeline. Any branch failure aborts the operation entirely. This is appropriate when

48
00:04:39.359 --> 00:04:46.000
all branches are required for a correct result. Second, proceed with partial results. Continue

49
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.679
with what succeeded and flag what failed. This works when partial results are still useful and

50
00:04:51.679 --> 00:04:59.760
the pipeline can degrade gracefully. Third, retry failed branches individually. Redispatch only the

51
00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:05.519
branches that errored rather than restarting the entire fan-out. Whichever strategy you choose,

52
00:05:05.519 --> 00:05:12.640
it has to be explicit. Silently dropping failed branches and continuing as if they succeeded is

53
00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:18.239
always the wrong answer. Silent failures introduce incorrect results that are extremely hard to

54
00:05:18.239 --> 00:05:24.239
diagnose after the fact. How fan-in merges results depends on the pipeline's purpose.

55
00:05:24.799 --> 00:05:31.600
Three common strategies. Voting. Multiple branches answer the same question and the majority result

56
00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:38.160
wins. This is useful when you want resilience against individual branch errors or inconsistencies.

57
00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:43.600
Concatenation. Branches produce independent outputs that are joined into a single list.

58
00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:50.399
Works when each branch handles a distinct segment of the input. Structured aggregation. Each branch

59
00:05:50.399 --> 00:05:56.480
contributes a named field to a shared output object. Useful when branches each compute a

60
00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:01.920
different attribute of the same entity. The key design point is to choose the merge strategy

61
00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:07.200
before designing the branches. The strategy shapes what each branch is expected to return,

62
00:06:07.279 --> 00:06:13.760
which shapes its entire design. Parallel execution cuts latency but raises token spend.

63
00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:20.000
When three branches run simultaneously, you're consuming roughly three times the tokens per second

64
00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:26.239
compared to running the same work sequentially. The total token cost is the sum across all branches,

65
00:06:26.239 --> 00:06:32.559
not just the longest one. Rate limits and quotas can also constrain how much concurrency is actually

66
00:06:32.559 --> 00:06:38.720
achievable in practice. If your API tier limits you to a certain number of concurrent requests,

67
00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:43.839
you may not get the latency improvement you expect from a large fan out. Model both dimensions

68
00:06:43.839 --> 00:06:50.320
explicitly before choosing parallel over sequential. The latency win is real, but so

69
00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:56.160
is the cost increase and the added complexity of synchronization and partial failure handling.

70
00:06:56.959 --> 00:07:02.559
So the bad outcome in production is a branch quietly writing over state another branch is

71
00:07:02.559 --> 00:07:08.640
still reading, and nobody catches it until an answer comes back subtly wrong. That's why

72
00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:14.640
independence gets tested before the dispatch, not after results land. When a branch fails,

73
00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:21.359
decide out loud what happens to the rest, because a silently dropped result does more damage than

74
00:07:21.359 --> 00:07:26.000
a slow one. That's where we'll wrap this one up. I'll see you in the next lesson.

